userStories.description:
The auto-memory system (`backend/src/lib/auto-memory.ts`) inserts memories into the `memories` table at three locations (lines ~161, ~337, ~565) but NEVER emits `memory_created` WAL events to the SSE hub. This means the embedding pipeline and graph populator never learn about new memories.

NOTE: `broadcastAll` and `WalEvent` are ALREADY imported at line 14 (`import { broadcastAll, type WalEvent } from './sse-hub'`). Do NOT add a duplicate import.

After each successful `INSERT INTO memories` call:
1. Change each INSERT to use `RETURNING id` and capture the returned row ID via `queryOne<{ id: string }>`.
2. After each INSERT succeeds, persist a WAL event AND broadcast it:
```typescript
const memRow = await queryOne<{ id: string }>(
  `INSERT INTO memories (...) VALUES (...) RETURNING id`,
  [...params]
);
if (memRow) {
  await execute(
    `INSERT INTO wal_events (event_type, workspace_id, spec_id, story_id, payload) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5)`,
    ['memory_created', workspaceId, sourceSpecId || null, storyId || null, JSON.stringify({ id: memRow.id, text: entry.content, category: entry.category })]
  );
  broadcastAll({
    event_type: 'memory_created',
    workspace_id: workspaceId,
    spec_id: sourceSpecId || null,
    story_id: storyId || null,
    payload: JSON.stringify({ id: memRow.id, text: entry.content, category: entry.category }),
  });
}
```
3. Apply to all THREE insert locations: `synthesizeStoryMemory()` (~line 161), `synthesizeSpecMemory()` (~line 337), `synthesizeRunMemory()` (~line 565).
4. Each INSERT currently includes an `embedding::vector` column with pre-computed embeddings. Preserve this — do NOT remove the embedding computation. Only add RETURNING id + WAL event emission.
5. Wrap WAL event emission in try/catch so a WAL failure doesn't prevent the memory from being saved.
